diagnosis_text:
The network is currently fully healthy. Cross-rack connectivity between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is working — pings complete with 0% packet loss and a traceroute confirms the 3-hop data path: leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_1 → leaf_router_0_1. All five BGP sessions are Established, all interfaces are UP, and no iptables rules block any traffic.

The one noteworthy control-plane observation is that `super_spine_router_0` consistently selects `spine_router_0_1`'s paths as best for both leaf prefixes (10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24), which causes spine_router_0_1 to receive zero routes from super_spine via standard BGP AS-PATH loop prevention (super_spine returns spine_0_1's own AS 65101 in the path). This creates an asymmetric load where spine_router_0_0 is entirely unused for forwarding and acts as a cold standby. This asymmetry is a benign side-effect of BGP path-selection tiebreaking (likely "oldest eBGP path" in the absence of `bgp best-path compare-routerid`) and does not break connectivity because both leaf routers also receive the remote prefix via spine_0_0 as a valid backup path.

The user's reported inability to reach the other rack was almost certainly a transient outage during BGP convergence in the 1–2 minutes immediately following a lab restart, before route entries were installed on the leaf routers. No configuration change is required; the network self-recovered as designed once BGP fully converged.
=== END ===